course: physics
lesson: 35
title: Pulleys, gears and big machines
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At school assembly someone pulls the flagpole rope down and the flag climbs up: a single wheel at the top, a pulley, simply turns your downward pull into an upward lift.
explain: Thread a rope through several pulleys and the load hangs on many lengths of rope that share its weight, so a builder pulls with much less force, but must pull much more rope.
explain: Gears are toothed wheels that turn each other: a small gear driving a big one turns it slowly with a strong twist, a big one driving a small one spins it fast, and two gears side by side always turn opposite ways.
explain: Cranes and lifts use a heavy counterweight like a seesaw partner, so the motor only has to lift the difference between the load and the weight.
explain: Every one of these machines swaps force for distance or speed; none of them gives energy for free.
fact: A single pulley changes the direction of a pull, so you can pull down, using your own weight, to lift something up.
fact: With several pulleys, more lengths of rope share the load, so you pull with less force but must pull much more rope.
fact: A crane uses pulleys, a long arm and a heavy counterweight that balances the load like a seesaw, and it has a load limit for each distance along the arm so it does not tip over.
fact: The gears on a bike are toothed wheels joined by the chain: a small gear at the back makes the wheel turn faster, a big one makes pedalling easier.
fact: Gears in a clock let one motor turn the minute hand twelve times round for each single turn of the hour hand.
fact: Two meshing gears always turn in opposite directions, and a third gear in between makes the last one turn the same way as the first.
fact: A lift is pulled up by cables over a wheel, with a counterweight going down as it goes up; lifts have several cables and safety brakes, so falling is almost impossible.
fact: An escalator is a long loop of steps pulled round by a motor and gears, and the steps flatten at the ends so you can step on and off.
fact: A water wheel at an old mill is turned by flowing water, and its axle drives gears that turn heavy millstones to grind flour.
fact: A car jack uses a screw or a liquid pump so that many small easy turns of a handle add up to lifting a whole car.
fact: You must never go under a car held up only by a jack, because it can slip.
exercise: Why is a pulley at the top of a flagpole handy? | It turns your downward pull into an upward lift | It makes the flag much lighter | It stops the wind from blowing the flag off the pole | It pulls the flag up by itself
exercise: Why is pulling down on a rope often easier than lifting up? | You can use your own weight to help | Ropes are stronger when pulled down | Gravity is weaker when you pull down | Your arms are longer going down
exercise: How do several pulleys make a heavy load easier to lift? | More lengths of rope share the weight | The pulleys make the load lighter | The wheels push the load from below | The rope gets thicker with more pulleys
exercise: What do you give up when pulleys make lifting easier? | You must pull much more rope | You must pull much harder instead | You must lift the load twice over | You give up nothing at all, it is free
exercise: Why does a crane have a big heavy block on its short arm? | It balances the load like a seesaw so the crane does not tip | It makes the crane look strong | It stores fuel for the engine | It stops birds landing on it
exercise: Why does a crane have a load limit for each distance along its arm? | Further out, the same load could tip it over | Further out, the loads somehow get lighter and lighter | The arm is thinner at the end | The rope is shorter at the end
exercise: What are the gears on a bike? | Toothed wheels joined by the chain | Springs hidden away inside the pedals | Small motors tucked inside the wheels | Brakes that slow the chain when you stop
exercise: Which back gear makes pedalling uphill easier? | A big gear | A small gear | No gear at all | Any gear is the same
exercise: How can one clock motor turn two hands at different speeds? | Gears of different sizes slow the turning down by different amounts | Each hand has its own battery | The hour hand is heavier and slower | The minute hand is pushed by a spring
exercise: How many times does the minute hand go round while the hour hand goes round once? | Twelve times | Sixty times | Twice | Twenty-four times
exercise: Two gears side by side are meshed. If the first turns clockwise, which way does the second turn? | Anticlockwise | Clockwise | It does not turn | It wobbles back and forth
exercise: What does a third gear placed in the middle of a row change? | The last gear turns the same way as the first | The last gear stops turning | All three of the gears then turn the same way round | The first gear turns backwards
exercise: Why do lifts have counterweights? | The weight balances the lift, so the motor only lifts the difference | The weight stops the lift from going too high | The weight is there to hold the doors shut | The weight is spare in case the lift breaks
exercise: Why do escalator steps flatten out at the ends? | So you can step on and off safely | To fold the steps away for the night | To make the escalator go faster | So the motor can rest
exercise: What did old water mills do? | Ground grain into flour with millstones | Made electricity to light up the big cities | Pumped water into houses | Cleaned the water in the river
exercise: How can a car jack lift a whole car with a small handle? | Many small easy turns add up to one big lift | The handle is made of a super-strong metal that pushes harder | The car is much lighter than it looks | A hidden motor does all the lifting
test: oi, the car's on the jack, just crawl under and grab my ball. fine? | No, never go under a car held only by a jack; it can slip | Yes, jacks never fail | Yes, if you are quick | Yes, if the engine is off
test: A builder hooks his rope through four pulleys instead of one. Why does he have to pull so much rope? | Less force means pulling over a longer distance | The extra pulleys make the rope stretchy | He is pulling the wrong end | Four pulleys make the load heavier, so he needs more rope
test: In a cartoon, a crane lifts a whole ship far out on its arm with no counterweight and stays perfectly upright. What's wrong? | A real crane would tip over without balance | Cranes can lift anything at all | Nothing, a real crane never tips over whatever it lifts | Ships are lighter than air
test: your mum says lifts can fall like in films. should you panic? | No, lifts have several cables and safety brakes | Yes, lifts fall all the time | Yes, they hang on only one thin cable that can snap | No, lifts float on air
test: Jas is cycling up a steep hill and her legs are dying. What should she do? | Change to an easier gear | Change to the hardest gear | Stop pedalling and coast | Pump up her tyres
test: Three gears are in a row. The first turns clockwise. Which way does the third turn? | Clockwise | Anticlockwise | It does not turn | It turns both ways at once
test: What would happen if a clock's hour hand were joined straight to the minute hand with no gears? | Both hands would turn at the same speed | The clock would run backwards instead of forwards | The hour hand would stop and never move again | The clock would chime every few minutes all day
test: Why did villagers long ago build their mill next to a stream? | Flowing water turned the wheel that ground the flour | The miller needed water to drink | Flour must be washed after grinding | Streams keep millstones cold
test: What's the clever trick that lets one motor run a whole escalator? | A long loop of steps is pulled round by a motor and gears | Each step has its own small engine | People's weight pushes the steps up | Magnets lift each step
test: Why can you raise the school flag with one hand even though the flag is high up? | The pulley lets you pull down to lift it up | The flag is pulled up high by the wind on its own | The pole stretches upwards when you pull the rope | The rope is magnetic and slides up the metal pole
test: Why can a small motor raise a heavy lift full of people? | The counterweight balances most of the weight | The lift is filled with helium | The cables pull upward on their own without any motor | People weigh less in lifts
exam: Identify the principal function of a single fixed pulley. | To alter the direction in which a force is applied | To reduce the mass of the load | To increase the energy which is available to the operator | To prevent the rope from stretching
exam: Ascertain the direction of rotation of a gear meshed directly with a second gear rotating clockwise. | Anticlockwise | Clockwise | It remains stationary | It alternates in direction
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of the counterweight of a lift? | It balances the car so that the motor need raise only the difference in weight | It prevents the car from ascending beyond the uppermost floor | It secures the doors in a closed position | It is held in reserve against the failure of the car
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a system of several pulleys. | The effort is diminished, but a greater length of rope must be drawn | The effort is increased, but less rope need be drawn | Both the effort and the rope drawn are diminished | The load is rendered lighter in itself
